package clwd:secrets@0.1.0;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DECISION 1 — an unauthorised secret is ABSENT, not denied.
//
// There is no `list`, and that is the smaller half of the design. The larger
// half is that this interface never performs a lookup against a store at all.
//
// The host resolves every secret an instance may see BEFORE the guest starts,
// and injects them into the instance's own state. `get` reads that map and
// nothing else. A secret the instance was not given is not refused by a check
// that could be wrong, or gated by a policy that could be misconfigured — it
// is not present, and there is no code path from this interface to anything
// that holds it.
//
//   The manifest DECLARES; the store AUTHORISES. The injection set is the
//   intersection. A manifest naming a secret the store will not resolve gets
//   nothing: a manifest can only narrow what is asked for, never grant.
//
// Consequences worth stating:
//
//   * `get` cannot fail. Resolution already happened, at spawn; if it had
//     failed the instance would never have started. There is nothing left to
//     go wrong at call time, so there is no error case.
//
//   * `none` means "you do not have this", covering both "no such secret
//     exists anywhere" and "this exists but is not yours" — deliberately
//     indistinguishable. Not by a rule someone must remember to follow, but
//     because they are the same value. There is no variant to add by accident
//     and no branch that could leak the difference. Distinguishing them would
//     rebuild, one answer at a time, the enumeration that removing `list`
//     was meant to prevent.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Read-only access to the secrets this app was granted.
interface broker {
    /// The value of `name`, or `none` if this app does not have it.
    ///
    /// `none` does not distinguish "no such secret" from "not yours", and must
    /// not be treated as evidence either way — see DECISION 1. An app that
    /// branches on the difference is branching on something this interface
    /// does not tell it.
    ///
    /// Values are bytes rather than a string: a private key, a certificate or
    /// a raw symmetric key is not necessarily valid UTF-8, and an interface
    /// that cannot carry those is one nobody discovers is wrong until they
    /// try. Callers holding a textual secret decode it themselves.
    get: func(name: string) -> option<list<u8>>;
}
